    "review": "## Code Review Report\n\n### Summary of Findings\nThis review evaluates the proposed modification to `DefaultDriverService.delete(Long driverId)` to ensure it correctly persists the soft-delete flag by saving the driver through `driverRepository`. The implementation aligns with the goal and does not introduce any new issues. No critical or high severity findings were identified.\n\n### Merge Readiness\nThe code change is **approved**. It adheres to the specified requirement of persisting the soft-delete flag in the `delete` method without altering its signature or annotations. There are no identified bugs, style violations, or architectural misalignments. The modification is minimal and focused, directly addressing the issue described in the goal.\n\n### Details\n- **Method `delete` in `DefaultDriverService`**: The method now correctly calls `driverRepository.save(driverDO)` after setting `driverDO.setDeleted(true)`, ensuring the soft-delete flag is persisted.\n- **No changes to method signature or annotations**: As required, no modifications were made to the method's signature, `@Transactional` or `@Override` annotations.\n- **No modifications to other methods or files**: The change strictly adheres to the scope defined in the task.\n\n### How to Run the Application\nThe provided code change is a single-line addition (`driverRepository.save(driverDO);`) within the `delete` method of `DefaultDriverService.java`. To compile and test the application:\n\n1. **Compile the project**:\n   ```bash\n   mvn clean compile\n   ```\n\n2. **Run tests (if applicable)**:\n   ```bash\n   mvn test\n   ```\n\n3. **Start the Spring Boot application**:\n   ```bash\n   mvn spring-boot:run\n   ```\n\nThe `delete` method can be verified by invoking it through a REST endpoint or unit test that checks for persistence of the `deleted` flag in the database. Note that the exact REST endpoints or controller mappings are not determinable from the supplied evidence, as per guidelines 6 and 7.",
